WEBVTT

NOTE Cue timings are estimated from sentence length and pauses in the audio.

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.870
Welcome to the Concilium Security Risk Brief for Thursday, October twenty second.

2
00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:12.474
Five minutes of security coverage from the last twenty four hours, to increase your situational awareness without decreasing your productivity.

3
00:00:12.474 --> 00:00:15.947
Here's what matters today.

4
00:00:15.947 --> 00:00:17.660
We start in Saudi Arabia.

5
00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:29.395
The State Department has issued an ordered departure for non-emergency US government employees and their family members in Saudi Arabia only, and called a report of departures across the wider Gulf inaccurate.

6
00:00:29.395 --> 00:00:35.423
It says Houthi attacks on civilian airports and other critical infrastructure have caused civilian casualties and severe travel disruptions.

7
00:00:35.423 --> 00:00:49.059
Reuters reports Saudi civil aviation says one person was killed and eight injured in an attack on King Fahd airport, and the UK Foreign Office says King Khalid Airport continues to be targeted.

8
00:00:49.059 --> 00:01:01.279
Dawn, citing state media, reports two people were killed by falling shrapnel when a drone was shot down over an Aramco facility, and the coalition says it downed six drones launched toward cities including Medina, with debris killing two.

9
00:01:01.279 --> 00:01:04.212
The Houthis deny launching drones toward Medina.

10
00:01:04.212 --> 00:01:08.794
Reuters also reports more people in Saudi Arabia are avoiding air travel.

11
00:01:08.794 --> 00:01:11.216
Next, two hostage releases.

12
00:01:11.216 --> 00:01:19.558
Radio Free Europe and Reuters report Russia has released seventy four year old US citizen Stephen Hubbard, who had been held since twenty twenty two.

13
00:01:19.558 --> 00:01:28.451
And Reuters reports two women from Austria and Switzerland, freed after eighteen months in Niger, have reached Benghazi in Libya.

14
00:01:28.451 --> 00:01:42.699
On Iran, the Critical Threats Project and the Institute for the Study of War say Iran may have used more accurate systems, likely anti-ship cruise missiles or infrared guided drones, to attack commercial shipping in the Strait of Hormuz over the past two weeks.

15
00:01:42.699 --> 00:01:51.518
Dawn reports Iran announced it targeted four ships, and the Revolutionary Guards threatened to hit US bases in retaliation for Hormuz escorts.

16
00:01:51.518 --> 00:01:59.343
In Taiwan, Reuters reports a land warning is in effect for Typhoon Champi, and more than one hundred flights have been cancelled.

17
00:01:59.343 --> 00:02:08.406
Australia says the storm is causing severe winds, heavy rain and rough seas, including in Taipei, and advises checking flight status before travel.

18
00:02:08.406 --> 00:02:21.913
On cyber, Microsoft says the Chinese state-aligned group Storm zero nine forty used an AI penetration testing tool to compromise the IT services firm ConnectWise, and targeted hundreds of organizations globally.

19
00:02:21.913 --> 00:02:24.326
A few updates on stories we've covered.

20
00:02:24.326 --> 00:02:30.190
On the PHP vulnerabilities, cyber agencies in Europe, Australia, Japan and Britain urged users to patch or take websites offline.

21
00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:41.275
Reuters reports some government websites in Europe went offline, the Institute for the Study of War took its own site offline as a precaution, and Asian governments have stepped up their defenses.

22
00:02:41.275 --> 00:02:49.796
On Ebola in Nigeria, Australia says Nigeria has introduced strict entry requirements, including an online health declaration form before travel.

23
00:02:49.796 --> 00:03:02.636
The UK Foreign Office added Ebola health information to its Nigeria advice, and the Africa Centres for Disease Control urged countries to strengthen preparedness.

24
00:03:02.636 --> 00:03:08.359
In Ethiopia, the UK Foreign Office updated its advice on conflict in the north of the country, including Tigray.

25
00:03:08.359 --> 00:03:18.822
In Pakistan, Dawn, citing police, reports fifteen to twenty armed men set up a roadblock and stopped a passenger bus near Dera Ismail Khan on the night of October twentieth.

26
00:03:18.822 --> 00:03:25.026
Global Decipher reports all of the abducted passengers have been recovered.

27
00:03:25.026 --> 00:03:33.743
And in North Korea, Reuters reports Kim Yo Jong said loudspeakers aimed at South Korea will be on standby along the entire border within days.

28
00:03:33.743 --> 00:03:35.948
Now a few quick notes by region.

29
00:03:35.948 --> 00:03:42.923
In the Middle East and North Africa, Reuters reports Egyptian President Sisi said Egypt would defend Saudi territory but ruled out an attack on Yemen.

30
00:03:42.923 --> 00:03:48.712
Masked men tried to storm Libya's financial oversight body.

31
00:03:48.712 --> 00:03:54.446
And US congressional candidate Brad Lander was detained by the Israeli military in the West Bank.

32
00:03:54.446 --> 00:04:01.868
The UN humanitarian office says nearly two hundred sixty thousand people in Yemen have been displaced by fighting since August, half of them in Taiz.

33
00:04:01.868 --> 00:04:09.088
In Africa, Reuters reports Burkina Faso's parliament voted to restore the death penalty.

34
00:04:09.088 --> 00:04:17.671
In Europe, Reuters reports an explosion injured four people in Athens, and Germany's military warned of a significant Russian sabotage threat.

35
00:04:17.671 --> 00:04:22.809
In Italy, retailers warned of food shortages caused by a truck drivers' protest, though truckers in the south have since eased their protests.

36
00:04:22.809 --> 00:04:26.054
France reported its first human case of Shamonda virus.

37
00:04:26.054 --> 00:04:38.506
And in Ukraine, Reuters reports Russian strikes killed elderly Ukrainians, as Russia launched cruise missiles and one hundred eighty six drones overnight.

38
00:04:38.506 --> 00:04:39.932
That's today's brief.

39
00:04:39.932 --> 00:04:48.055
These security developments come from government travel advisories and trusted analysts, curated by the Concilium Security Risk Management Team.

40
00:04:48.055 --> 00:04:56.274
The brief summarizes public reporting and isn't a substitute for your own risk assessment, so check the original source before making travel decisions.

41
00:04:56.274 --> 00:04:58.384
This brief is digitally narrated.

42
00:04:58.384 --> 00:04:59.317
Questions?

43
00:04:59.317 --> 00:05:02.774
Reach Concilium at info at concilium dot U.S.

44
00:05:02.774 --> 00:05:05.060
Thanks for listening, and stay safe.
